oups sometimes called  lineages  clads variant groupings  and the two main ways to do that are in  silico testing  where the laboratory or the assay  designer will monitor known mutations  and check it against the primary probe  regions of their  testing and make sure there's no problem  with the binding and the primers or  probes  and then we have in vitro testing where  actual clinical  uh samples with known strain variants  are tested  or or unknown strain variants are tested  to  identify if if those are um  meeting the criteria for a variant for  sars kovi ii  now there's a lot of surveillance going  on in the united states and around the  globe for the viral mutations  in the united states about six to seven  percent  of the virus isolates are being  sequenced  the virus mutates at a rate of about two  mutations per month  and they are mostly single nucleotide  and the cdc puts out a  bar graph that shows the recent variance  and the changes in them  and so you can see here the b.1.1.7  that gained in popularity and and these  are um  available usually on the state and  federal public health websites  this is a on the right is a map of the  vaccination rate so right now  you know we're fighting the vaccination  rate  hoping it will become higher so these  variants  don't get out of control the only way to  stop the variants  is to get people vaccinated but there's  still a relatively low vaccination rate  in certain states in certain  geographical pockets and that is why we  have to continue  to be vigilant for the viral  mutations cdc classifies the variants  and the mutations into  three major categories um we've talked  about  all the mutations thousands of them that  have no  known consequence and we won't we won't  belabor that any further  but there is um a variance of interest a  variant concern  and a variant of high consequence all  three  of the variant categories show reduced  uh effectiveness to the monoclonal  antibody combinations for therapy  and the convalescent and post  vaccination serum  the variant of concern is is more severe  or  more of a of a worry than the variant of  interest it also has  increased transmission increased  severity  as does the variant of high consequence  and the demonstrated failure of diagnose  diagnostics so here we come into the  range where we are being impacted in the  clinical laboratory  and then a variant of high concern  there's really none of these yet  but it would be called if there was  reduced effectiveness  to any of the therapeutics and  medications that are there or miss with  the vaccine  so these are examples of the sars-kov2  nomenclature  and the associated protein changes or  amino acid changes  and then up top you'll see  the abbreviations for variance of  concern or variants of interest the voi  so the alpha variance of b1  b.1.1.7 origin  originated in the uk and  these are the amino acid changes that  you would see  in that particular variant the  the beta variant is the b.1.351 the  south african  similar and other changes there  these are these are describing some of  the common amino acids and how they're  characterized but it's  it's the whole sequence that will  actually tell you  uh specifically  and confirm that you have these variants  in your population  then we have the gamma variant the delta  variant the epsilon zeta  eta iota and kappa so many of these  are you'll see some of them are listed  more than twice the one that's  originated in brazil has the p1 and the  p2  and they are named differently because  of their genetic dissimilarity  so here you have the who nomenclature  which is the greek you have the next  strain nomenclature  which is the amino acid then you have  the pangolina  which is commonly used to identify those  strains so this  this will give you a little bit of a  help when you're trying to sort out  which variants they're talking about  it's it's a little complicated because  there's more than one  name for each of thes